To distribute .applescript text files to fellow OS X 10.4 users or to convert UTF-8 encoded files to .applescript text files suitable for 
\f1\b Script Editor
\f0\b0 , you should take into consideration the following remarks.\
\
If you save a .applescript text file from 
\f1\b Script Editor
\f0\b0 , then normally it is saved as text in the system encoding. Unfortunately this causes problems when distributing these types of files to systems where the default encoding is not the same as the one where they were created.\
\
When opening .applescript files, 
\f1\b Script Editor
\f0\b0  also expects them to have the system encoding unless they are encoded as UTF-16. But the languages supported in these OSA components are more than likely to have scripts written with Unicode UTF-8 encoding since that is the standard of the modern scripting world. They also must be converted to Unicode UTF-16 so 
\f1\b Script Editor
\f0\b0  will not get confused about which encoding the file uses.\
\
\
To deal with these problems, The guidelines are:\
\
\
1. 
\f1\b \cf3 For converting 
\f0\b0 \cf2 "
\f1\b xxx.txt
\f0\b0 "
\f1\b \cf4  \cf3 encoded in UTF-8 to\cf4  
\f0\b0 \cf2 "
\f1\b xxx.applescript
\f0\b0 " in 
\f1\b Terminal
\f0\b0  use:\
\
	
\f1\b iconv -f UTF-8 -t UTF-16 xxx.txt > xxx.applescript
\f0\b0 \
\
Now "
\f1\b xxx.applescript
\f0\b0 " is good for using in Script Editor to save as .app, .scptd, or .scpt.\
\
\
\
2. 
\f1\b \cf3 After "Save As\'c9" from an\cf4  
\f0\b0 \cf2 .
\f1\b app
\f0\b0 , .
\f1\b scptd
\f0\b0 , or .
\f1\b scpt
\f0\b0  in Script Editor to "
\f1\b xxx.applescript
\f0\b0 " use:\
\
	
\f1\b iconv -t UTF-16 xxx.applescript > yyy.applescript
\f0\b0 	\
\
Now "
\f1\b yyy.applescript
\f0\b0 " is good for international distribution.\
\
